 <46025606+Ken5998@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 15:34:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] fix(posix): do not read the instruction on an instruction
 abort

On aarch64 the exception handler reads the instruction at the program
counter to tell a load from a store. When the program counter itself is
what faulted (a call through a null or garbage function pointer), there
is no instruction to read, so the handler crashed inside the signal
handler instead of passing the fault on.

An instruction abort (ESR exception class 0b10000x) is now reported as
an access violation of unknown kind. Without the ESR in the signal frame
(qemu-user), a fault address equal to the program counter means the
same.
---
 sdk/src/core/exception_handler_posix.cpp | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sdk/src/core/exception_handler_posix.cpp b/sdk/src/core/exception_handler_posix.cpp
index 2c89a15..a98659c 100644
--- a/sdk/src/core/exception_handler_posix.cpp
+++ b/sdk/src/core/exception_handler_posix.cpp
@@ -226,6 +226,13 @@ static void ExceptionHandlerCallback(int signal_number, siginfo_t* signal_info,
         access_violation_operation = (mcontext_esr->esr & (UINT64_C(1) << 6))
                                          ? Exception::AccessViolationOperation::kWrite
                                          : Exception::AccessViolationOperation::kRead;
+      } else if (mcontext_esr ? ((mcontext_esr->esr >> 26) & 0b111110) == 0b100000
+                              : reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(signal_info->si_addr) == mcontext.pc) {
+        // An Instruction Abort (EC 0b100000 or 0b100001; without the ESR, a
+        // fault on the program counter itself): the program counter is not
+        // executable, for instance after a call through a null function
+        // pointer, so there is no instruction there to read.
+        access_violation_operation = Exception::AccessViolationOperation::kUnknown;
       } else {
         // Determine the memory access direction based on which instruction has
         // requested it.
